  "predictive_services_discussion": "A major pattern change will develop today as a trough aloft drops south across the West with a strong leading cold front. Winds ahead of the front may gust as high as 50 mph across the high terrain and lee slopes from southern California to Montana. Temperatures will still be hot and relative humidity still dry and a wide swath of near critical to critical fire weather is expected for Montana and Wyoming and near the Sierra Front. Hot and dry conditions with east winds gusting 15 to 35 mph will bring a critical burn environment to the lower Mississippi Valley out ahead of Hurricane Isaias. By late Friday night, Isaias is anticipated to make landfall bringing heavy rain, strong winds, and storm surge to the Gulf Coast. Cooler temperatures, moderated relative humidity, and light rain are expected across Washington state and western Oregon. National Predictive Services Outlook (7-Day Significant Fire Potential Outlook) National Weather Service, Storm Prediction Center: Fire Weather Outlook Map and discussion National Weather Service: Fire Weather Hazards Map National Weather Service, Weather Prediction Center: National Forecast Chart and Short Range Forecast Discussion 6 Minutes for Safety topics can be found here.",
